The box is built. What is left needs you in person:

1. Register the signing key on GitHub, from your workstation:
     gh auth refresh -h github.com -s admin:ssh_signing_key
     ssh {{ agent_user }}@{{ inventory_hostname }} cat .ssh/id_ed25519_signing.pub > {{ inventory_hostname }}-signing.pub
     gh ssh-key add {{ inventory_hostname }}-signing.pub --type signing --title "{{ inventory_hostname }} agent signing"

2. Sign each agent server in to Claude and start it once by hand, as {{ agent_user }} on the box. The first start asks questions a service cannot answer: answer y to trust the folder and to enable Remote Control, wait until it says it is connected, press Ctrl+C, then hand over to the service. An environment in a container is started first and waits, inside its container, until it is signed in; then its server asks to enable Remote Control in its tmux session (leave with Ctrl+B then D).
{% for server in environments %}
{% set dir = server.config_dir | default(agent_home ~ '/.claude') %}
{% set env = 'CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=' ~ dir ~ ' ' if dir != agent_home ~ '/.claude' else '' %}
   {{ server.name }}{{ ' (in its container)' if server.runtime | default('host', true) == 'container' else '' }}:
{% if server.runtime | default('host', true) == 'container' %}
     systemctl --user start agent-env-{{ server.name }}.service
     podman exec -it agent-env-{{ server.name }} claude
       (sign in, trust the folder, run /mcp and authorise each connector that asks, then /exit)
     podman exec -it agent-env-{{ server.name }} tmux -L agent-{{ server.name }} attach
       (answer y to enable Remote Control, wait until it says it is connected, then Ctrl+B then D)
{% else %}
     {{ env }}claude
       (sign in, run /mcp and authorise each connector that asks, then /exit)
     cd {{ nucleus_root }} && {{ env }}claude remote-control --spawn worktree --no-create-session-in-dir --no-chrome
     systemctl --user start agent-server@{{ server.name }}.service
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

{% set whoami = day2_watch_whoami | default({}) %}
{% if watch_target == 'none' %}
3. Nucarium Watch is off (watch_target: none), so nothing leaves the box. To see what Watch would show, as {{ agent_user }} on the box:
     nucleated status
{% elif whoami.rc | default(5) == 0 %}
{% set login = whoami.stdout | from_json %}
3. Nucarium Watch: this box is signed in as "{{ login.machine_name | default(inventory_hostname) }}" and reports to {{ login.account_name | default('your account') }}. It appears at {{ watch_console_url }} within a minute.
{% else %}
3. {{ 'Nucarium Watch no longer accepts this box\'s token. Sign it in again' if whoami.rc | default(5) == 6 else 'Sign the box in to Nucarium Watch' }}, as {{ agent_user }} on the box:
     nucleate login
   It prints a short code such as WDJB-MJHT, the address {{ watch_console_url }}/activate and a link that already carries the code, then waits. Open either on your phone or laptop, sign in, choose this machine or name a new one, and approve. The code lasts 15 minutes. The box then says what was granted, "{{ inventory_hostname }} can now report to <your account>. It can read nothing.", and appears in Watch within a minute. Watch sees which agent is doing what, where and since when, never what it was told or what it wrote.
{% endif %}

4. Check the daemon, as {{ agent_user }} on the box:
     nucleated check
     nucleated status

5. The disk-header backup is on your workstation in out/{{ inventory_hostname }}-luks-header.img. Attach it to the disk passphrase in your password manager, then delete the file.
